AB  - Scalable and automated machine learning (ML) models have been developed recently as a result of the integration of cloud-native technologies and DevOps principles. Unresolved challenges like excessive energy consumption, poor lifecycle coordination, fragmented multi-cloud interoperability, lack of dynamic resource allocation and a lack of uniform governance and observability remain unsolved. This literature review proposes Adaptive Cloud DevOps Orchestration Framework (ACDOF), a novel framework that combines a predictive orchestration engine for dynamically fluctuating machine learning workloads, carbon efficient and cost aware scheduling mechanisms, self-healing automation for hybrid and cross-cloud environments and a unified governance framework for observability, artifact versioning and compliance monitoring. The primary goal of ACDOF aims to make ML systems more flexible and enhance performance under changing workload conditions. By combining DevOps ideas with centralized monitoring capabilities, it also improves reproducibility, enhances portability between cloud environments, and increases system transparency. This review demonstrates that current pipelines are reactive, require manual intervention and are unable to adjust to fluctuating ML operations. It is based on insights from previous studies on cloud centric and MLOps research. By implementing an integrated orchestration layer that integrates cross-cloud decision making, sustainability aware scheduling, predictive workload intelligence, and unified governance-driven lifecycle management of ML workloads, ACDOF overcomes these limitations. This design transforms ML operations from static, tool-dependent pipelines into a continuously self-optimizing system capable of responding to real-time performance, cost and energy conditions. By bridging the gaps in scalability, observability and operational resilience, ACDOF proposes a practical framework towards building intelligent, sustainable and reliable ML infrastructures suitable for next generation enterprise deployments.
ER  - 

